WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.125
Welcome. This class turns AI-assisted
coding and analysis into a

2
00:00:04.125 --> 00:00:08.250
controlled evidence loop. You will inspect
the real system before

3
00:00:08.250 --> 00:00:12.375
proposing work, define what may change and
what must remain

4
00:00:12.375 --> 00:00:16.500
true, make one reviewable increment at a
time, and test

5
00:00:16.500 --> 00:00:20.625
the result outside the model's
explanation. You will also preserve

6
00:00:20.625 --> 00:00:24.750
failures and recover narrowly instead of
hiding evidence. Success means

7
00:00:24.750 --> 00:00:28.875
you can reproduce the change, show why it
is correct,

8
00:00:28.875 --> 00:00:33.000
and reverse it safely.

9
00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:37.067
Prepare a work order before asking for
code, a query,

10
00:00:37.067 --> 00:00:41.133
or a conclusion. For code, state the
behavior to change,

11
00:00:41.133 --> 00:00:45.200
relevant files and interfaces,
constraints, acceptance checks, and

12
00:00:45.200 --> 00:00:49.267
invariants that must not change. For
analysis, state the decision

13
00:00:49.267 --> 00:00:53.333
or question, data fields, units, time
period, population, permitted

14
00:00:53.333 --> 00:00:57.400
transformations, and reconciliation
checks. Inspect the actual repository,

15
00:00:57.400 --> 00:01:01.467
schema, sample, documentation, and current
tests. Do not let a

16
00:01:01.467 --> 00:01:05.533
model guess their contents. Choose a
workspace appropriate to risk:

17
00:01:05.533 --> 00:01:09.600
a branch or isolated copy for code, and a

18
00:01:09.600 --> 00:01:13.667
sandbox or read-only source for data.
Remove credentials and unnecessary

19
00:01:13.667 --> 00:01:17.733
personal or restricted information. Name
actions that require separate

20
00:01:17.733 --> 00:01:21.800
authorization, including package
installation, network access, production

21
00:01:21.800 --> 00:01:25.867
writes, deletion, or permission changes.
Finally, record a rollback path.

22
00:01:25.867 --> 00:01:29.933
A bounded work order prevents a useful
suggestion from silently

23
00:01:29.933 --> 00:01:34.000
becoming authority to change the system.

24
00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:38.308
Suppose a report drops orders that have no
matching customer

25
00:01:38.308 --> 00:01:42.615
row. The bounded question is not, fix the
analytics. It

26
00:01:42.615 --> 00:01:46.923
is: identify unmatched orders and report
their value without changing

27
00:01:46.923 --> 00:01:51.231
the accepted revenue total. I inspect the
order and customer

28
00:01:51.231 --> 00:01:55.538
keys, row grain, null rules, currency
units, existing query, and

29
00:01:55.538 --> 00:01:59.846
reconciliation test. I work on a copy of
the query

30
00:01:59.846 --> 00:02:04.154
against approved sample data. The
invariant is that the original

31
00:02:04.154 --> 00:02:08.462
order count and amount remain traceable.
The checks compare source

32
00:02:08.462 --> 00:02:12.769
counts, unique keys, unmatched keys,
joined counts, and totals. Production

33
00:02:12.769 --> 00:02:17.077
data writes and schema changes are outside
scope. Rollback means

34
00:02:17.077 --> 00:02:21.385
restoring the prior query and saved
results. This definition makes

35
00:02:21.385 --> 00:02:25.692
a join proposal testable and prevents an
unrelated model-generated cleanup

36
00:02:25.692 --> 00:02:30.000
from entering the work.

37
00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:34.200
Execute one coherent increment at a time.
Ask for a

38
00:02:34.200 --> 00:02:38.400
short plan tied to the inspected inputs,
then verify that

39
00:02:38.400 --> 00:02:42.600
each step remains inside the work order.
For code, change

40
00:02:42.600 --> 00:02:46.800
one behavior, inspect the diff, and run
the nearest check

41
00:02:46.800 --> 00:02:51.000
before expanding scope. Prefer established
project patterns. Reject unrequested

42
00:02:51.000 --> 00:02:55.200
frameworks, dependencies, permissions,
configuration changes, and broad rewrites.

43
00:02:55.200 --> 00:02:59.400
For analysis, preserve raw inputs and
write transformations in an

44
00:02:59.400 --> 00:03:03.600
inspectable sequence. Record filters,
joins, calculations, missing-value

45
00:03:03.600 --> 00:03:07.800
decisions, units, and assumptions. Keep
intermediate row counts and totals

46
00:03:07.800 --> 00:03:12.000
so later conclusions can be traced
backward. If a tool

47
00:03:12.000 --> 00:03:16.200
proposes a command, query, or file change,
read it before

48
00:03:16.200 --> 00:03:20.400
execution and confirm the target,
environment, and effect. Separate planning

49
00:03:20.400 --> 00:03:24.600
authority from execution authority:
generating a command is not permission

50
00:03:24.600 --> 00:03:28.800
to run it. Small increments localize
errors and make rollback

51
00:03:28.800 --> 00:03:33.000
a routine step instead of a crisis.

52
00:03:33.000 --> 00:03:36.750
Choose a low-risk code or data task. Write
one observable

53
00:03:36.750 --> 00:03:40.500
outcome, one invariant that must remain
true, one check that

54
00:03:40.500 --> 00:03:44.250
proves the outcome, and one action that is
explicitly outside

55
00:03:44.250 --> 00:03:48.000
your authority.

56
00:05:18.000 --> 00:05:22.286
Verify with the system and data, not with
the model's

57
00:05:22.286 --> 00:05:26.571
confidence. For code, run the formatter,
type checker, focused unit

58
00:05:26.571 --> 00:05:30.857
tests, relevant integration tests, and
production build that match the

59
00:05:30.857 --> 00:05:35.143
change. Exercise the success path, an
important failure path, and

60
00:05:35.143 --> 00:05:39.429
the actual user-visible behavior. Review
the final diff for unrelated

61
00:05:39.429 --> 00:05:43.714
files, disabled checks, weakened
assertions, exposed secrets, new

62
00:05:43.714 --> 00:05:48.000
dependencies, and generated artifacts. For
analysis, validate field types,

63
00:05:48.000 --> 00:05:52.286
missingness, units, value ranges,
duplicates, keys, join cardinality,

64
00:05:52.286 --> 00:05:56.571
unmatched rows, filters, outliers, and
before-and-after totals. Confirm that the

65
00:05:56.571 --> 00:06:00.857
sample represents the population needed
for the conclusion. Recalculate a

66
00:06:00.857 --> 00:06:05.143
small case independently. A successful
query proves only that the

67
00:06:05.143 --> 00:06:09.429
query executed. A passing test proves only
what it actually

68
00:06:09.429 --> 00:06:13.714
asserts. Link each acceptance criterion to
direct output, and record

69
00:06:13.714 --> 00:06:18.000
gaps rather than converting them into
confident prose.

70
00:06:18.000 --> 00:06:22.091
Use a fictional miniature join before
judging the checkpoint. Orders

71
00:06:22.091 --> 00:06:26.182
contains three rows: O1, customer C1, 100
cents; O2, C2,

72
00:06:26.182 --> 00:06:30.273
200 cents; O3, C1, 300 cents. Customers
wrongly contains C1,

73
00:06:30.273 --> 00:06:34.364
Alice; C1, Alicia; C2, Bob. Before
joining, orders have three

74
00:06:34.364 --> 00:06:38.455
rows and total 600 cents. An inner join on

75
00:06:38.455 --> 00:06:42.545
customer identifier returns five rows
because each C1 order matches

76
00:06:42.545 --> 00:06:46.636
twice and a duplicated total of 1000
cents. After deduplicating

77
00:06:46.636 --> 00:06:50.727
customers to one row per identifier, the
join returns three

78
00:06:50.727 --> 00:06:54.818
rows and 600 cents. The exact checks are
key uniqueness,

79
00:06:54.818 --> 00:06:58.909
joined row count, distinct order count,
unmatched orders, and amount

80
00:06:58.909 --> 00:07:03.000
reconciliation.

81
00:07:03.000 --> 00:07:07.167
Checkpoint. The order table has one
thousand three hundred rows.

82
00:07:07.167 --> 00:07:11.333
The customer table has one thousand rows.
After joining on

83
00:07:11.333 --> 00:07:15.500
customer name, the result has one thousand
six hundred fifty

84
00:07:15.500 --> 00:07:19.667
rows. The generated explanation says the
increase is normal because

85
00:07:19.667 --> 00:07:23.833
customers can place multiple orders. Is
that sufficient evidence, and

86
00:07:23.833 --> 00:07:28.000
what should you check next?

87
00:08:28.000 --> 00:08:32.273
The explanation is not sufficient.
Multiple orders already exist as

88
00:08:32.273 --> 00:08:36.545
separate order rows; they do not by
themselves explain why

89
00:08:36.545 --> 00:08:40.818
a join created three hundred fifty
additional rows. Inspect the

90
00:08:40.818 --> 00:08:45.091
grain and uniqueness of both keys.
Customer name may be

91
00:08:45.091 --> 00:08:49.364
duplicated or may identify different
people. Count matched and unmatched

92
00:08:49.364 --> 00:08:53.636
keys, test for many-to-many combinations,
compare distinct order identifiers, and

93
00:08:53.636 --> 00:08:57.909
reconcile amounts before and after the
join. If you accepted

94
00:08:57.909 --> 00:09:02.182
the result because the query succeeded,
revise your criterion: successful

95
00:09:02.182 --> 00:09:06.455
execution is not data validity. If the key
is unsafe,

96
00:09:06.455 --> 00:09:10.727
use an approved stable identifier or
report that the available

97
00:09:10.727 --> 00:09:15.000
data cannot support the join.

98
00:09:15.000 --> 00:09:19.357
Preserve failure evidence before
attempting repair. Save the command or

99
00:09:19.357 --> 00:09:23.714
query, exact error, relevant input,
environment, and smallest reproduction.

100
00:09:23.714 --> 00:09:28.071
Classify the responsible layer:
expectation, code, data, dependency,

101
00:09:28.071 --> 00:09:32.429
permission, network, configuration, or
environment. Return to the last verified

102
00:09:32.429 --> 00:09:36.786
increment and change the narrowest
responsible layer. Do not delete

103
00:09:36.786 --> 00:09:41.143
a test, weaken an assertion, discard
inconvenient rows, increase a

104
00:09:41.143 --> 00:09:45.500
timeout, or regenerate the entire solution
merely to obtain green

105
00:09:45.500 --> 00:09:49.857
output. Rerun the failed check,
neighboring regression checks, and the

106
00:09:49.857 --> 00:09:54.214
original acceptance checks. If the
environment is uncertain, stop and

107
00:09:54.214 --> 00:09:58.571
compare versions and configuration before
changing code. If recovery requires

108
00:09:58.571 --> 00:10:02.929
destructive action, production access, new
permissions, or cost beyond the

109
00:10:02.929 --> 00:10:07.286
work order, preserve state and escalate.
Use version control or

110
00:10:07.286 --> 00:10:11.643
a verified backup to reverse unsafe work,
but do not

111
00:10:11.643 --> 00:10:16.000
erase logs or artifacts needed to
understand the failure.

112
00:10:16.000 --> 00:10:20.143
Review security, privacy, and downstream
impact before accepting the work.

113
00:10:20.143 --> 00:10:24.286
Inspect authentication, authorization,
input validation, output encoding,

114
00:10:24.286 --> 00:10:28.429
secrets, logs, dependencies, network
calls, file targets, data exposure, error

115
00:10:28.429 --> 00:10:32.571
handling, and failure defaults. Generated
code may compile while bypassing

116
00:10:32.571 --> 00:10:36.714
an account state or logging a credential.
A statistically correct

117
00:10:36.714 --> 00:10:40.857
transformation may still expose personal
data, erase a protected group,

118
00:10:40.857 --> 00:10:45.000
use incompatible units, or support a
conclusion beyond the sample.

119
00:10:45.000 --> 00:10:49.143
Scan the diff and outputs for sensitive
material. Confirm the

120
00:10:49.143 --> 00:10:53.286
reviewer, deployment boundary, backup,
monitoring, and rollback evidence.

121
00:10:53.286 --> 00:10:57.429
Destructive, external, costly, or
permission-expanding actions require the

122
00:10:57.429 --> 00:11:01.571
authorized workflow and explicit approval;
model confidence never substitutes for

123
00:11:01.571 --> 00:11:05.714
authority. Record the work order,
inspected inputs, diff or query,

124
00:11:05.714 --> 00:11:09.857
check outputs, failure-and-recovery
evidence, limitations, reviewer, and

125
00:11:09.857 --> 00:11:14.000
disposition. Commit or publish only the
verified scoped result.

126
00:11:14.000 --> 00:11:18.385
The baseline policy depends on processing
order. First require the

127
00:11:18.385 --> 00:11:22.769
exact five-field header. Python's
DictReader exposes the detected header

128
00:11:22.769 --> 00:11:27.154
through reader.fieldnames, so the program
can compare the names and

129
00:11:27.154 --> 00:11:31.538
their order with the required schema. For
every row, build

130
00:11:31.538 --> 00:11:35.923
a tuple from all five raw values and check

131
00:11:35.923 --> 00:11:40.308
it against previously seen rows before
validation. A repeated tuple

132
00:11:40.308 --> 00:11:44.692
increments ignored_duplicates and stops
processing that row. A new row

133
00:11:44.692 --> 00:11:49.077
needs nonempty invoice and customer
identifiers, kind equal to charge

134
00:11:49.077 --> 00:11:53.462
or refund, and a positive amount
containing only ASCII decimal

135
00:11:53.462 --> 00:11:57.846
digits. Reject a different row that reuses
an accepted invoice

136
00:11:57.846 --> 00:12:02.231
identifier. Rejected rows reserve nothing.
Accepted charges add to gross;

137
00:12:02.231 --> 00:12:06.615
accepted refunds add only to refunds; net
equals gross minus

138
00:12:06.615 --> 00:12:11.000
refunds. Print the six labels in the
required fixed order.

139
00:12:11.000 --> 00:12:15.250
Read transactions.csv in file order.
DictReader produces each data row

140
00:12:15.250 --> 00:12:19.500
as a mapping keyed by the header fields.
A100 is

141
00:12:19.500 --> 00:12:23.750
an accepted 1250-cent charge. The first
A101 is an accepted

142
00:12:23.750 --> 00:12:28.000
250-cent refund referencing A100. The next
A101 repeats all five

143
00:12:28.000 --> 00:12:32.250
raw fields, so it is one ignored
duplicate, not an

144
00:12:32.250 --> 00:12:36.500
invoice conflict. A102 adds 3000 cents to
gross, and A103

145
00:12:36.500 --> 00:12:40.750
adds 1000. A104 has an empty amount and is

146
00:12:40.750 --> 00:12:45.000
rejected. Therefore six data rows
reconcile as four accepted plus

147
00:12:45.000 --> 00:12:49.250
one duplicate plus one rejected. Gross is
1250 plus 3000

148
00:12:49.250 --> 00:12:53.500
plus 1000, or 5250. Refunds are 250, and
net is

149
00:12:53.500 --> 00:12:57.750
5000. Under the supplied baseline rule,
refund references are informational

150
00:12:57.750 --> 00:13:02.000
and may be empty safely.

151
00:13:02.000 --> 00:13:06.364
In analyze_buggy.py, validation succeeds
before accounting reaches the quoted

152
00:13:06.364 --> 00:13:10.727
condition `kind == "charge" or "refund"`.
Python does not compare

153
00:13:10.727 --> 00:13:15.091
kind with both strings. It evaluates `kind
== "charge"` first.

154
00:13:15.091 --> 00:13:19.455
For a refund, that comparison is false, so
the or

155
00:13:19.455 --> 00:13:23.818
expression evaluates the standalone
nonempty string `"refund"`. A nonempty

156
00:13:23.818 --> 00:13:28.182
string has a true truth value, making the
complete condition

157
00:13:28.182 --> 00:13:32.545
true. Thus every accepted refund enters
gross. A separate equality

158
00:13:32.545 --> 00:13:36.909
check also enters it in refunds. The
250-cent refund is

159
00:13:36.909 --> 00:13:41.273
counted in both totals, producing gross
5500 and net 5250.

160
00:13:41.273 --> 00:13:45.636
The defect is classification, not CSV
parsing, duplicate handling, or

161
00:13:45.636 --> 00:13:50.000
arithmetic. Preserve this exact condition
as diagnosis evidence first.

162
00:13:50.000 --> 00:13:54.091
Translate the policy into observable
behavior. Test one compares all

163
00:13:54.091 --> 00:13:58.182
six original output lines exactly. Test
two checks gross 5250

164
00:13:58.182 --> 00:14:02.273
and refunds 250 separately. Test three
